<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Ali Pasha, Mehmet Ali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Ali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was an Albanian officer of the Ottoman army who became governor of Egypt in 1805 and founded a dynasty that ruled until 1952. He destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army and state industries, crushed the first Saudi state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> in 1818 and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from 1820. His wars against the sultan in the 1830s nearly toppled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali of Egypt" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Couder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ModernEgypt,_Muhammad_Ali_by_Auguste_Couder,_BAP_17996.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1820 <b>Muhammad Ali</b>, the Ottoman viceroy of Egypt, sent an army under his son Ismail Kamil Pasha to conquer the Sudan, seeking gold, slaves for his new army and the elimination of Mamluk refugees who had settled at Dongola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Modern Egypt was built by <b>Muhammad Ali Pasha</b>, who seized power in 1805 and founded a dynasty that ruled until 1952.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Muhammad Ali of Egypt</b>, governor from 1805, built a conscript army and a centralised state that needed doctors, engineers, officers and translators.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Provincial governors (ayans) and local leaders such as <b>Muhammad Ali of Egypt</b> increasingly defied the centre.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mehmet Ali</b> Ağca shot and seriously wounded the Pope as he greeted crowds in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Muhammad Ali</b>, an Albanian officer sent to fight the French, exploited the chaos after the French occupation of Egypt and was recognised as Ottoman governor in 1805.</blockquote><a href="/state/egypt-under-muhammad-ali">Egypt under Muhammad Ali</a></li></ul></section>
